card.statement:
The triple $(M; \rho,\gamma)$ is called a weak module over $(A,m,\eta,L,\mu,\alpha,\beta)$ if it satisfies the following conditions:

- [(1)] the pair $(M,\rho)$ is an $A$-module;

- [(2)] the pair $(M,\gamma)$ is an $L$-module;

- [(3)] the following compatible condition is satisfied:

\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzpicture}[anchorbase]
\draw[bmod] (0.25, 0) -- (0.5, 0.25);
\draw[bmod] (0, 0) -- (0, 0.25) -- (0.25, 0.5);
\draw[bmod] (0.75, 0) -- (0.5, 0.25) -- (0.25, 0.5) -- (0.25, 0.75);
\btoken{0.5, 0.25}{west}{\rho};
\btoken{0.25, 0.5}{west}{\gamma};
\end{tikzpicture}
\ =\
\begin{tikzpicture}[anchorbase]
\draw[bmod] (0.5, 0) -- (0.25, 0.25);
\draw[bmod] (0, 0) -- (0.25, 0.25) -- (0.5, 0.5);
\draw[bmod] (0.75, 0) -- (0.75, 0.25) -- (0.5, 0.5) -- (0.5, 0.75);
\btoken[2px]{0.5, 0.5}{west}{\rho};
\btoken[2px]{0.25, 0.25}{east}{\alpha};
\end{tikzpicture}
\ +\
\begin{tikzpicture}[anchorbase]
\draw[bmod] (0, -0.25) -- (0.25, 0) -- (0.5, 0.25);
\draw[bmod] (0.25, -0.25) -- (0, 0) -- (0, 0.25) -- (0.25, 0.5);
\draw[bmod] (0.75, -0.25) -- (0.75, 0) -- (0.5, 0.25) -- (0.25, 0.5) -- (0.25, 0.75);
\btoken{0.5, 0.25}{west}{\gamma};
\btoken[2px]{0.25, 0.5}{west}{\rho};
\end{tikzpicture}\ .
\end{equation}
